{
  "slug": "implement-queue-using-stacks",
  "title": "Implement Queue using Stacks",
  "topic": "stack",
  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "pattern": "Two stacks with amortised O(1) transfer",
  "statement": "A queue is first-in first-out while a stack is last-in first-out, so a queue has to be simulated on top of stack storage. Replay the encoded queue operations below in order and report what the queue returns.\n\nEach element of `operations` is one operation:\n\n- a positive value `v` is `push(v)`, which appends `v` at the back of the queue;\n- `0` is `pop()`, which removes the element at the front of the queue and outputs it;\n- `-1` is `peek()`, which outputs the element at the front of the queue without removing it.\n\nThe queue starts empty. A `pop` or `peek` on an empty queue outputs `-1` and changes nothing. Return the outputs of every `pop` and `peek` call in the order the calls happen; a `push` produces no output.\n\nConstraints: `1 <= operations.length <= 200` and `-1 <= operations[i] <= 100`.\n",
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": "operations = [-1]",
      "output": "[-1]",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    },
    {
      "input": "operations = [30, 94, 70, 67, 32]",
      "output": "[]",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    }
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "Function signature: `function solve(operations: number[])`.",
    "Inputs are JSON-serializable values satisfying the bounds stated above."
  ],
  "hints": [
    "With one stack you get the back of the queue for free, but the front is buried at the bottom.",
    "Pouring a stack onto a second stack reverses it, so the front of the queue ends up on top. What if you kept that reversed stack instead of rebuilding it every time?",
    "Keep an input stack and an output stack. Push onto the input stack; when a pop or peek needs the front and the output stack is empty, empty the entire input stack into it. Each element moves between the two stacks at most once."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The brute force keeps a single stack and pays full price on every dequeue: it pours the whole stack onto a temporary stack, reads the front (which is now on top), and pours the survivors back. The optimal splits storage by role into an input stack and an output stack. Pushes always land on the input stack, and the output stack is refilled by draining the input stack only when it is empty. Draining reverses the order, so the top of the output stack is always the front of the queue, and each element crosses between the two stacks at most once over the whole run, which is why the work is amortised O(1) per call even though a single drain is O(n).",
    "why_optimal": "Each of the n operations has to be replayed in order, so \u03a9(n) total work is unavoidable, and reading every operation exactly once achieves it. The two-stack version does that with amortised O(1) per operation, which is the best possible. The brute force is \u0398(n\u00b2) in the worst case: an alternating push, pop, push, pop pattern rebuilds the entire stack on every dequeue. With n \u2264 200 both are fast, but the amortised version is the intended design.",
    "pitfalls": "Draining the input stack even when the output stack still holds elements, which reverses an order that was already correct and returns the wrong value; after draining, still treating the queue as non-empty when both stacks are empty, so `undefined`/`None` is emitted instead of -1; restoring the temporary stack in forward order, which flips the front and back; in Python writing `if op:` to test for a push, which treats 0 as false and silently swallows every pop."
  },
